1
00:00:05,478 --> 00:00:10,879
Betimes in the morning I was up and out. It was too early yet to go to Miss Havisham’s,

2
00:00:10,879 --> 00:00:17,478
so I loitered into the country on Miss Havisham’s side of town,—which was not Joe’s side; I could go

3
00:00:17,478 --> 00:00:24,359
there to-morrow,—thinking about my patroness, and painting brilliant pictures of her plans for me.

4
00:00:24,359 --> 00:00:29,637
She had adopted Estella, she had as good as adopted me, and it could not fail to be her

5
00:00:29,638 --> 00:00:35,638
intention to bring us together. She reserved it for me to restore the desolate house, admit

6
00:00:35,638 --> 00:00:41,680
the sunshine into the dark rooms, set the clocks a-going and the cold hearths a-blazing, tear down

7
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:48,840
the cobwebs, destroy the vermin,—in short, do all the shining deeds of the young Knight of romance,

8
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:55,599
and marry the Princess. I had stopped to look at the house as I passed; and its seared red brick

9
00:00:55,599 --> 00:01:01,199
walls, blocked windows, and strong green ivy clasping even the stacks of chimneys with its

10
00:01:01,199 --> 00:01:08,637
twigs and tendons, as if with sinewy old arms, had made up a rich attractive mystery, of which I was

11
00:01:08,638 --> 00:01:16,680
the hero. Estella was the inspiration of it, and the heart of it, of course. But, though she had

12
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:22,680
taken such strong possession of me, though my fancy and my hope were so set upon her, though

13
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:28,798
her influence on my boyish life and character had been all-powerful, I did not, even that romantic

14
00:01:28,798 --> 00:01:35,879
morning, invest her with any attributes save those she possessed. I mention this in this place,

15
00:01:35,879 --> 00:01:42,398
of a fixed purpose, because it is the clue by which I am to be followed into my poor labyrinth.

16
00:01:42,398 --> 00:01:48,797
According to my experience, the conventional notion of a lover cannot be always true. The

17
00:01:48,798 --> 00:01:55,680
unqualified truth is, that when I loved Estella with the love of a man, I loved her simply because

18
00:01:55,680 --> 00:02:04,359
I found her irresistible. Once for all; I knew to my sorrow, often and often, if not always, that I

19
00:02:04,359 --> 00:02:12,958
loved her against reason, against promise, against peace, against hope, against happiness, against

20
00:02:12,958 --> 00:02:20,680
all discouragement that could be. Once for all; I loved her none the less because I knew it, and it

21
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:27,638
had no more influence in restraining me than if I had devoutly believed her to be human perfection.

22
00:02:27,638 --> 00:02:34,238
I so shaped out my walk as to arrive at the gate at my old time. When I had rung at the

23
00:02:34,238 --> 00:02:39,758
bell with an unsteady hand, I turned my back upon the gate, while I tried to get my breath

24
00:02:39,759 --> 00:02:46,840
and keep the beating of my heart moderately quiet. I heard the side-door open, and steps come across

25
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:53,039
the courtyard; but I pretended not to hear, even when the gate swung on its rusty hinges.

26
00:02:53,840 --> 00:03:00,719
Being at last touched on the shoulder, I started and turned. I started much more naturally then,

27
00:03:00,719 --> 00:03:06,317
to find myself confronted by a man in a sober grey dress. The last man I should

28
00:03:06,317 --> 00:03:10,359
have expected to see in that place of porter at Miss Havisham’s door.

29
00:03:10,359 --> 00:03:11,838
“Orlick!”

30
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:16,478
“Ah, young master, there’s more changes than yours. But come in,

31
00:03:16,478 --> 00:03:21,238
come in. It’s opposed to my orders to hold the gate open.”

32
00:03:21,238 --> 00:03:27,519
I entered and he swung it, and locked it, and took the key out. “Yes!” said he,

33
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:34,039
facing round, after doggedly preceding me a few steps towards the house. “Here I am!”

34
00:03:34,039 --> 00:03:35,759
“How did you come here?”

35
00:03:35,759 --> 00:03:40,879
“I come here,” he retorted, “on my legs. I had my box brought alongside me in a barrow.”

36
00:03:40,879 --> 00:03:42,680
“Are you here for good?”

37
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:46,118
“I ain’t here for harm, young master, I suppose?”

38
00:03:46,118 --> 00:03:51,199
I was not so sure of that. I had leisure to entertain the retort in my mind,

39
00:03:51,199 --> 00:03:58,080
while he slowly lifted his heavy glance from the pavement, up my legs and arms, to my face.

40
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:01,638
“Then you have left the forge?” I said.

41
00:04:01,638 --> 00:04:05,199
“Do this look like a forge?” replied Orlick,

42
00:04:05,199 --> 00:04:11,759
sending his glance all round him with an air of injury. “Now, do it look like it?”

43
00:04:11,759 --> 00:04:15,879
I asked him how long he had left Gargery’s forge?

44
00:04:15,879 --> 00:04:22,958
“One day is so like another here,” he replied, “that I don’t know without casting it up. However,

45
00:04:23,478 --> 00:04:26,798
I come here some time since you left.”

46
00:04:26,798 --> 00:04:29,840
“I could have told you that, Orlick.”

47
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:35,680
“Ah!” said he, dryly. “But then you’ve got to be a scholar.”

48
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:39,919
By this time we had come to the house, where I found his room to be one just within the

49
00:04:39,919 --> 00:04:45,638
side-door, with a little window in it looking on the courtyard. In its small proportions,

50
00:04:45,638 --> 00:04:50,919
it was not unlike the kind of place usually assigned to a gate-porter in Paris. Certain

51
00:04:50,919 --> 00:04:55,958
keys were hanging on the wall, to which he now added the gate key; and his patchwork-covered

52
00:04:55,958 --> 00:05:02,759
bed was in a little inner division or recess. The whole had a slovenly, confined, and sleepy look,

53
00:05:02,759 --> 00:05:08,478
like a cage for a human dormouse; while he, looming dark and heavy in the shadow of a corner

54
00:05:08,478 --> 00:05:15,478
by the window, looked like the human dormouse for whom it was fitted up,—as indeed he was.

55
00:05:15,478 --> 00:05:21,039
“I never saw this room before,” I remarked; “but there used to be no Porter here.”

56
00:05:21,039 --> 00:05:26,000
“No,” said he; “not till it got about that there was no protection on the premises, and it come to

57
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,958
be considered dangerous, with convicts and Tag and Rag and Bobtail going up and down. And then

58
00:05:31,958 --> 00:05:35,840
I was recommended to the place as a man who could give another man as good as he brought,

59
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:42,118
and I took it. It’s easier than bellowsing and hammering.—That’s loaded, that is.”

60
00:05:42,118 --> 00:05:45,199
My eye had been caught by a gun with a brass-bound stock over the

61
00:05:45,199 --> 00:05:52,840
chimney-piece, and his eye had followed mine. “Well,” said I, not desirous of more conversation,

62
00:05:53,478 --> 00:05:58,238
“shall I go up to Miss Havisham?” “Burn me, if I know!” he retorted,

63
00:05:58,238 --> 00:06:04,118
first stretching himself and then shaking himself; “my orders ends here, young master. I give this

64
00:06:04,118 --> 00:06:10,638
here bell a rap with this here hammer, and you go on along the passage till you meet somebody.”

65
00:06:10,638 --> 00:06:13,238
“I am expected, I believe?”

66
00:06:13,238 --> 00:06:17,558
“Burn me twice over, if I can say!” said he.

67
00:06:17,559 --> 00:06:23,278
Upon that, I turned down the long passage which I had first trodden in my thick boots, and he

68
00:06:23,278 --> 00:06:29,798
made his bell sound. At the end of the passage, while the bell was still reverberating, I found

69
00:06:29,798 --> 00:06:36,879
Sarah Pocket, who appeared to have now become constitutionally green and yellow by reason of me.

70
00:06:36,879 --> 00:06:41,438
“Oh!” said she. “You, is it, Mr. Pip?”

71
00:06:41,438 --> 00:06:47,278
“It is, Miss Pocket. I am glad to tell you that Mr. Pocket and family are all well.”

72
00:06:47,278 --> 00:06:51,798
“Are they any wiser?” said Sarah, with a dismal shake of the head;

73
00:06:51,798 --> 00:06:58,520
“they had better be wiser, than well. Ah, Matthew, Matthew! You know your way, sir?”

74
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:04,879
Tolerably, for I had gone up the staircase in the dark, many a time. I ascended it now,

75
00:07:04,879 --> 00:07:08,798
in lighter boots than of yore, and tapped in my old way at the

76
00:07:08,798 --> 00:07:16,798
door of Miss Havisham’s room. “Pip’s rap,” I heard her say, immediately; “come in, Pip.”

77
00:07:16,798 --> 00:07:20,478
She was in her chair near the old table, in the old dress,

78
00:07:20,478 --> 00:07:24,559
with her two hands crossed on her stick, her chin resting on them,

79
00:07:24,559 --> 00:07:30,958
and her eyes on the fire. Sitting near her, with the white shoe, that had never been worn,

80
00:07:30,958 --> 00:07:38,438
in her hand, and her head bent as she looked at it, was an elegant lady whom I had never seen.

81
00:07:38,438 --> 00:07:43,159
“Come in, Pip,” Miss Havisham continued to mutter, without looking round or up;

82
00:07:43,159 --> 00:07:49,438
“come in, Pip, how do you do, Pip? so you kiss my hand as if I were a queen, eh?—Well?”

83
00:07:49,438 --> 00:07:55,598
She looked up at me suddenly, only moving her eyes, and repeated in a grimly playful manner,—

84
00:07:55,599 --> 00:07:56,919
“Well?”

85
00:07:56,919 --> 00:08:00,559
“I heard, Miss Havisham,” said I, rather at a loss,

86
00:08:00,559 --> 00:08:04,879
“that you were so kind as to wish me to come and see you, and I came directly.”

87
00:08:04,879 --> 00:08:06,039
“Well?”

88
00:08:06,039 --> 00:08:10,840
The lady whom I had never seen before, lifted up her eyes and looked archly at me,

89
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:16,118
and then I saw that the eyes were Estella’s eyes. But she was so much changed,

90
00:08:16,118 --> 00:08:21,477
was so much more beautiful, so much more womanly, in all things winning admiration,

91
00:08:21,478 --> 00:08:28,599
had made such wonderful advance, that I seemed to have made none. I fancied, as I looked at her,

92
00:08:28,599 --> 00:08:33,719
that I slipped hopelessly back into the coarse and common boy again. O the sense

93
00:08:33,719 --> 00:08:40,759
of distance and disparity that came upon me, and the inaccessibility that came about her!

94
00:08:40,759 --> 00:08:46,238
She gave me her hand. I stammered something about the pleasure I felt in seeing her again,

95
00:08:46,238 --> 00:08:51,598
and about my having looked forward to it, for a long, long time.

96
00:08:51,599 --> 00:08:56,520
“Do you find her much changed, Pip?” asked Miss Havisham, with her greedy look,

97
00:08:56,520 --> 00:09:02,238
and striking her stick upon a chair that stood between them, as a sign to me to sit down there.

98
00:09:02,238 --> 00:09:07,359
“When I came in, Miss Havisham, I thought there was nothing of Estella in the face or figure;

99
00:09:07,359 --> 00:09:10,797
but now it all settles down so curiously into the old—”

100
00:09:10,798 --> 00:09:16,317
“What? You are not going to say into the old Estella?” Miss Havisham interrupted. “She was

101
00:09:16,317 --> 00:09:22,000
proud and insulting, and you wanted to go away from her. Don’t you remember?”

102
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:26,599
I said confusedly that that was long ago, and that I knew no better then,

103
00:09:26,599 --> 00:09:32,958
and the like. Estella smiled with perfect composure, and said she had no doubt of

104
00:09:32,958 --> 00:09:38,080
my having been quite right, and of her having been very disagreeable.

105
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:41,477
“Is he changed?” Miss Havisham asked her.

106
00:09:41,478 --> 00:09:44,520
“Very much,” said Estella, looking at me.

107
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:49,559
“Less coarse and common?” said Miss Havisham, playing with Estella’s hair.

108
00:09:49,559 --> 00:09:55,438
Estella laughed, and looked at the shoe in her hand, and laughed again, and looked at me,

109
00:09:55,438 --> 00:10:02,118
and put the shoe down. She treated me as a boy still, but she lured me on.

110
00:10:02,118 --> 00:10:08,080
We sat in the dreamy room among the old strange influences which had so wrought upon me,

111
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:14,080
and I learnt that she had but just come home from France, and that she was going to London.

112
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:20,159
Proud and wilful as of old, she had brought those qualities into such subjection to her beauty that

113
00:10:20,159 --> 00:10:27,039
it was impossible and out of nature—or I thought so—to separate them from her beauty. Truly it

114
00:10:27,039 --> 00:10:31,798
was impossible to dissociate her presence from all those wretched hankerings after money and

115
00:10:31,798 --> 00:10:36,879
gentility that had disturbed my boyhood,—from all those ill-regulated aspirations that had

116
00:10:36,879 --> 00:10:42,278
first made me ashamed of home and Joe,—from all those visions that had raised her face in the

117
00:10:42,278 --> 00:10:48,317
glowing fire, struck it out of the iron on the anvil, extracted it from the darkness of night

118
00:10:48,317 --> 00:10:55,598
to look in at the wooden window of the forge, and flit away. In a word, it was impossible

119
00:10:55,599 --> 00:11:05,000
for me to separate her, in the past or in the present, from the innermost life of my life.

120
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:11,000
It was settled that I should stay there all the rest of the day, and return to the hotel at night,

121
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:17,159
and to London to-morrow. When we had conversed for a while, Miss Havisham sent us two out to

122
00:11:17,159 --> 00:11:22,278
walk in the neglected garden: on our coming in by and by, she said, I should wheel her

123
00:11:22,278 --> 00:11:28,118
about a little, as in times of yore. So, Estella and I went out into the

124
00:11:28,118 --> 00:11:32,638
garden by the gate through which I had strayed to my encounter with the pale young gentleman,

125
00:11:32,638 --> 00:11:38,840
now Herbert; I, trembling in spirit and worshipping the very hem of her dress;

126
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:44,840
she, quite composed and most decidedly not worshipping the hem of mine. As we drew near

127
00:11:44,840 --> 00:11:49,919
to the place of encounter, she stopped and said,— “I must have been a singular little creature to

128
00:11:49,919 --> 00:11:56,238
hide and see that fight that day; but I did, and I enjoyed it very much.”

129
00:11:56,238 --> 00:11:58,718
“You rewarded me very much.”

130
00:11:58,719 --> 00:12:02,719
“Did I?” she replied, in an incidental and forgetful

131
00:12:02,719 --> 00:12:07,238
way. “I remember I entertained a great objection to your adversary,

132
00:12:07,238 --> 00:12:11,958
because I took it ill that he should be brought here to pester me with his company.”

133
00:12:11,958 --> 00:12:14,317
“He and I are great friends now.”

134
00:12:14,317 --> 00:12:19,718
“Are you? I think I recollect though, that you read with his father?”

135
00:12:19,719 --> 00:12:21,080
“Yes.”

136
00:12:21,080 --> 00:12:22,879
I made the admission with reluctance,

137
00:12:22,879 --> 00:12:29,118
for it seemed to have a boyish look, and she already treated me more than enough like a boy.

138
00:12:29,118 --> 00:12:36,000
“Since your change of fortune and prospects, you have changed your companions,” said Estella.

139
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,039
“Naturally,” said I.

140
00:12:38,039 --> 00:12:41,638
“And necessarily,” she added, in a haughty tone;

141
00:12:41,638 --> 00:12:47,039
“what was fit company for you once, would be quite unfit company for you now.”

142
00:12:47,039 --> 00:12:52,438
In my conscience, I doubt very much whether I had any lingering intention left of going to see Joe;

143
00:12:53,238 --> 00:12:57,558
but if I had, this observation put it to flight.

144
00:12:57,559 --> 00:13:03,317
“You had no idea of your impending good fortune, in those times?” said Estella,

145
00:13:03,317 --> 00:13:08,000
with a slight wave of her hand, signifying in the fighting times.

146
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:09,958
“Not the least.”

147
00:13:09,958 --> 00:13:14,719
The air of completeness and superiority with which she walked at my side,

148
00:13:14,719 --> 00:13:18,879
and the air of youthfulness and submission with which I walked at hers,

149
00:13:18,879 --> 00:13:24,359
made a contrast that I strongly felt. It would have rankled in me more than it did,

150
00:13:24,359 --> 00:13:31,519
if I had not regarded myself as eliciting it by being so set apart for her and assigned to her.

151
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:36,680
The garden was too overgrown and rank for walking in with ease, and after we had made the round of

152
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:43,478
it twice or thrice, we came out again into the brewery yard. I showed her to a nicety where I

153
00:13:43,478 --> 00:13:49,798
had seen her walking on the casks, that first old day, and she said, with a cold and careless look

154
00:13:49,798 --> 00:13:56,039
in that direction, “Did I?” I reminded her where she had come out of the house and given me my

155
00:13:56,039 --> 00:14:04,638
meat and drink, and she said, “I don’t remember.” “Not remember that you made me cry?” said I. “No,”

156
00:14:04,638 --> 00:14:11,478
said she, and shook her head and looked about her. I verily believe that her not remembering

157
00:14:11,478 --> 00:14:18,398
and not minding in the least, made me cry again, inwardly,—and that is the sharpest crying of all.

158
00:14:18,398 --> 00:14:23,718
“You must know,” said Estella, condescending to me as a brilliant and beautiful woman might,

159
00:14:23,719 --> 00:14:28,958
“that I have no heart,—if that has anything to do with my memory.”

160
00:14:28,958 --> 00:14:31,958
I got through some jargon to the effect that I took the liberty of

161
00:14:31,958 --> 00:14:37,879
doubting that. That I knew better. That there could be no such beauty without it.

162
00:14:37,879 --> 00:14:44,599
“Oh! I have a heart to be stabbed in or shot in, I have no doubt,” said Estella,

163
00:14:44,599 --> 00:14:48,478
“and of course if it ceased to beat I should cease to be. But you know what

164
00:14:48,478 --> 00:14:54,398
I mean. I have no softness there, no—sympathy—sentiment—nonsense.”

165
00:14:54,398 --> 00:14:58,317
What was it that was borne in upon my mind when she stood still and looked attentively

166
00:14:58,317 --> 00:15:05,598
at me? Anything that I had seen in Miss Havisham? No. In some of her looks and

167
00:15:05,599 --> 00:15:11,478
gestures there was that tinge of resemblance to Miss Havisham which may often be noticed to have

168
00:15:11,478 --> 00:15:18,520
been acquired by children, from grown person with whom they have been much associated and secluded,

169
00:15:18,520 --> 00:15:23,638
and which, when childhood is passed, will produce a remarkable occasional likeness of

170
00:15:23,638 --> 00:15:29,840
expression between faces that are otherwise quite different. And yet I could not trace this to Miss

171
00:15:29,840 --> 00:15:36,478
Havisham. I looked again, and though she was still looking at me, the suggestion was gone.

172
00:15:36,478 --> 00:15:38,278
What was it?

173
00:15:38,278 --> 00:15:44,199
“I am serious,” said Estella, not so much with a frown (for her brow was smooth) as

174
00:15:44,199 --> 00:15:49,199
with a darkening of her face; “if we are to be thrown much together,

175
00:15:49,199 --> 00:15:54,759
you had better believe it at once. No!” imperiously stopping me as I opened my

176
00:15:54,759 --> 00:16:01,520
lips. “I have not bestowed my tenderness anywhere. I have never had any such thing.”

177
00:16:01,520 --> 00:16:08,438
In another moment we were in the brewery, so long disused, and she pointed to the high gallery where

178
00:16:08,438 --> 00:16:14,359
I had seen her going out on that same first day, and told me she remembered to have been up there,

179
00:16:14,359 --> 00:16:22,278
and to have seen me standing scared below. As my eyes followed her white hand, again the same

180
00:16:22,278 --> 00:16:28,278
dim suggestion that I could not possibly grasp crossed me. My involuntary start occasioned

181
00:16:28,278 --> 00:16:35,317
her to lay her hand upon my arm. Instantly the ghost passed once more and was gone.

182
00:16:35,317 --> 00:16:37,118
What was it?

183
00:16:37,118 --> 00:16:42,000
“What is the matter?” asked Estella. “Are you scared again?”

184
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:47,680
“I should be, if I believed what you said just now,” I replied, to turn it off.

185
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:53,199
“Then you don’t? Very well. It is said, at any rate. Miss Havisham will soon be

186
00:16:53,199 --> 00:16:57,477
expecting you at your old post, though I think that might be laid aside now,

187
00:16:57,478 --> 00:17:03,520
with other old belongings. Let us make one more round of the garden, and then go in. Come! You

188
00:17:03,520 --> 00:17:09,478
shall not shed tears for my cruelty to-day; you shall be my Page, and give me your shoulder.”

189
00:17:09,478 --> 00:17:13,879
Her handsome dress had trailed upon the ground. She held it in one hand now,

190
00:17:13,879 --> 00:17:19,317
and with the other lightly touched my shoulder as we walked. We walked round the ruined garden

191
00:17:19,317 --> 00:17:25,958
twice or thrice more, and it was all in bloom for me. If the green and yellow growth of weed

192
00:17:25,958 --> 00:17:30,159
in the chinks of the old wall had been the most precious flowers that ever blew,

193
00:17:30,159 --> 00:17:34,759
it could not have been more cherished in my remembrance.

194
00:17:34,759 --> 00:17:39,000
There was no discrepancy of years between us to remove her far from me;

195
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:45,638
we were of nearly the same age, though of course the age told for more in her case than in mine;

196
00:17:45,638 --> 00:17:49,879
but the air of inaccessibility which her beauty and her manner gave her,

197
00:17:49,879 --> 00:17:53,278
tormented me in the midst of my delight, and at the height of the

198
00:17:53,278 --> 00:17:59,438
assurance I felt that our patroness had chosen us for one another. Wretched boy!

199
00:17:59,438 --> 00:18:04,519
At last we went back into the house, and there I heard, with surprise,

200
00:18:04,520 --> 00:18:08,599
that my guardian had come down to see Miss Havisham on business, and would come back

201
00:18:08,599 --> 00:18:14,759
to dinner. The old wintry branches of chandeliers in the room where the mouldering table was spread

202
00:18:14,759 --> 00:18:20,840
had been lighted while we were out, and Miss Havisham was in her chair and waiting for me.

203
00:18:20,840 --> 00:18:26,278
It was like pushing the chair itself back into the past, when we began the old slow

204
00:18:26,278 --> 00:18:32,238
circuit round about the ashes of the bridal feast. But, in the funereal room,

205
00:18:32,238 --> 00:18:37,118
with that figure of the grave fallen back in the chair fixing its eyes upon her,

206
00:18:37,118 --> 00:18:44,398
Estella looked more bright and beautiful than before, and I was under stronger enchantment.

207
00:18:44,398 --> 00:18:49,838
The time so melted away, that our early dinner-hour drew close at hand, and Estella

208
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:57,398
left us to prepare herself. We had stopped near the centre of the long table, and Miss Havisham,

209
00:18:57,398 --> 00:19:02,558
with one of her withered arms stretched out of the chair, rested that clenched hand upon the

210
00:19:02,559 --> 00:19:08,719
yellow cloth. As Estella looked back over her shoulder before going out at the door, Miss

211
00:19:08,719 --> 00:19:15,398
Havisham kissed that hand to her, with a ravenous intensity that was of its kind quite dreadful.

212
00:19:15,398 --> 00:19:22,317
Then, Estella being gone and we two left alone, she turned to me, and said in a whisper,—

213
00:19:22,317 --> 00:19:28,519
“Is she beautiful, graceful, well-grown? Do you admire her?”

214
00:19:28,520 --> 00:19:32,238
“Everybody must who sees her, Miss Havisham.”

215
00:19:32,238 --> 00:19:36,477
She drew an arm round my neck, and drew my head close down to hers as

216
00:19:36,478 --> 00:19:44,317
she sat in the chair. “Love her, love her, love her! How does she use you?”

217
00:19:44,317 --> 00:19:50,438
Before I could answer (if I could have answered so difficult a question at all) she repeated,

218
00:19:50,438 --> 00:19:54,558
“Love her, love her, love her! If she favours you,

219
00:19:54,559 --> 00:20:00,478
love her. If she wounds you, love her. If she tears your heart to pieces,—and as it

220
00:20:00,478 --> 00:20:07,958
gets older and stronger it will tear deeper,—love her, love her, love her!”

221
00:20:07,958 --> 00:20:13,159
Never had I seen such passionate eagerness as was joined to her utterance of these words. I

222
00:20:13,159 --> 00:20:18,558
could feel the muscles of the thin arm round my neck swell with the vehemence that possessed her.

223
00:20:18,559 --> 00:20:24,719
“Hear me, Pip! I adopted her, to be loved. I bred her and educated her,

224
00:20:24,719 --> 00:20:32,759
to be loved. I developed her into what she is, that she might be loved. Love her!”

225
00:20:32,759 --> 00:20:37,278
She said the word often enough, and there could be no doubt that she meant to say it;

226
00:20:37,278 --> 00:20:43,637
but if the often repeated word had been hate instead of love—despair—revenge—dire

227
00:20:43,638 --> 00:20:48,840
death—it could not have sounded from her lips more like a curse.

228
00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:55,398
“I’ll tell you,” said she, in the same hurried passionate whisper, “what real love is. It is

229
00:20:55,398 --> 00:21:01,958
blind devotion, unquestioning self-humiliation, utter submission, trust and belief against

230
00:21:01,958 --> 00:21:09,478
yourself and against the whole world, giving up your whole heart and soul to the smiter—as I did!”

231
00:21:09,478 --> 00:21:13,599
When she came to that, and to a wild cry that followed that,

232
00:21:13,599 --> 00:21:19,798
I caught her round the waist. For she rose up in the chair, in her shroud of a dress,

233
00:21:19,798 --> 00:21:27,398
and struck at the air as if she would as soon have struck herself against the wall and fallen dead.

234
00:21:27,398 --> 00:21:32,718
All this passed in a few seconds. As I drew her down into her chair,

235
00:21:32,719 --> 00:21:39,438
I was conscious of a scent that I knew, and turning, saw my guardian in the room.

236
00:21:39,438 --> 00:21:44,080
He always carried (I have not yet mentioned it, I think) a pocket-handkerchief of rich

237
00:21:44,080 --> 00:21:50,118
silk and of imposing proportions, which was of great value to him in his profession. I

238
00:21:50,118 --> 00:21:55,317
have seen him so terrify a client or a witness by ceremoniously unfolding this

239
00:21:55,317 --> 00:22:00,838
pocket-handkerchief as if he were immediately going to blow his nose, and then pausing,

240
00:22:00,840 --> 00:22:06,317
as if he knew he should not have time to do it before such client or witness committed himself,

241
00:22:06,317 --> 00:22:11,080
that the self-committal has followed directly, quite as a matter of course.

242
00:22:11,080 --> 00:22:16,519
When I saw him in the room he had this expressive pocket-handkerchief in both hands, and was looking

243
00:22:16,520 --> 00:22:24,238
at us. On meeting my eye, he said plainly, by a momentary and silent pause in that attitude,

244
00:22:24,238 --> 00:22:31,438
“Indeed? Singular!” and then put the handkerchief to its right use with wonderful effect.

245
00:22:31,438 --> 00:22:37,758
Miss Havisham had seen him as soon as I, and was (like everybody else) afraid of him. She

246
00:22:37,759 --> 00:22:42,759
made a strong attempt to compose herself, and stammered that he was as punctual as ever.

247
00:22:42,759 --> 00:22:47,359
“As punctual as ever,” he repeated, coming up to us. “(How do you do,

248
00:22:47,359 --> 00:22:54,039
Pip? Shall I give you a ride, Miss Havisham? Once round?) And so you are here, Pip?”

249
00:22:54,039 --> 00:22:57,798
I told him when I had arrived, and how Miss Havisham had wished me to come and

250
00:22:57,798 --> 00:23:04,359
see Estella. To which he replied, “Ah! Very fine young lady!” Then he pushed

251
00:23:04,359 --> 00:23:08,438
Miss Havisham in her chair before him, with one of his large hands,

252
00:23:08,438 --> 00:23:14,317
and put the other in his trousers-pocket as if the pocket were full of secrets.

253
00:23:14,317 --> 00:23:21,080
“Well, Pip! How often have you seen Miss Estella before?” said he, when he came to a stop.

254
00:23:21,080 --> 00:23:21,719
“How often?”

255
00:23:22,520 --> 00:23:26,759
“Ah! How many times? Ten thousand times?”

256
00:23:26,759 --> 00:23:29,199
“Oh! Certainly not so many.”

257
00:23:29,199 --> 00:23:30,477
“Twice?”

258
00:23:30,478 --> 00:23:34,359
“Jaggers,” interposed Miss Havisham, much to my relief,

259
00:23:34,359 --> 00:23:37,679
“leave my Pip alone, and go with him to your dinner.”

260
00:23:37,680 --> 00:23:42,798
He complied, and we groped our way down the dark stairs together. While we were still on

261
00:23:42,798 --> 00:23:46,879
our way to those detached apartments across the paved yard at the back,

262
00:23:46,879 --> 00:23:51,638
he asked me how often I had seen Miss Havisham eat and drink; offering me a breadth of choice,

263
00:23:52,278 --> 00:23:55,798
as usual, between a hundred times and once.

264
00:23:55,798 --> 00:23:58,958
I considered, and said, “Never.”

265
00:23:58,958 --> 00:24:04,080
“And never will, Pip,” he retorted, with a frowning smile. “She has never allowed

266
00:24:04,080 --> 00:24:08,719
herself to be seen doing either, since she lived this present life of hers.

267
00:24:08,719 --> 00:24:14,159
She wanders about in the night, and then lays hands on such food as she takes.”

268
00:24:14,159 --> 00:24:17,958
“Pray, sir,” said I, “may I ask you a question?”

269
00:24:17,958 --> 00:24:23,039
“You may,” said he, “and I may decline to answer it. Put your question.”

270
00:24:23,039 --> 00:24:27,638
“Estella’s name. Is it Havisham or—?” I had nothing to add.

271
00:24:27,638 --> 00:24:29,118
“Or what?” said he.

272
00:24:29,118 --> 00:24:30,798
“Is it Havisham?”

273
00:24:30,798 --> 00:24:33,000
“It is Havisham.”

274
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:39,118
This brought us to the dinner-table, where she and Sarah Pocket awaited us. Mr. Jaggers presided,

275
00:24:39,118 --> 00:24:45,159
Estella sat opposite to him, I faced my green and yellow friend. We dined very well,

276
00:24:45,159 --> 00:24:50,558
and were waited on by a maid-servant whom I had never seen in all my comings and goings,

277
00:24:50,559 --> 00:24:54,118
but who, for anything I know, had been in that mysterious house the

278
00:24:54,118 --> 00:25:00,519
whole time. After dinner a bottle of choice old port was placed before my guardian (he

279
00:25:00,520 --> 00:25:06,039
was evidently well acquainted with the vintage), and the two ladies left us.

280
00:25:06,039 --> 00:25:11,680
Anything to equal the determined reticence of Mr. Jaggers under that roof I never saw elsewhere,

281
00:25:11,680 --> 00:25:17,080
even in him. He kept his very looks to himself, and scarcely directed his eyes

282
00:25:17,080 --> 00:25:21,559
to Estella’s face once during dinner. When she spoke to him, he listened,

283
00:25:22,118 --> 00:25:29,159
and in due course answered, but never looked at her, that I could see. On the other hand,

284
00:25:29,159 --> 00:25:35,477
she often looked at him, with interest and curiosity, if not distrust, but his face never

285
00:25:35,478 --> 00:25:40,961
showed the least consciousness. Throughout dinner he took a dry delight in making Sarah

286
00:25:40,961 --> 00:25:47,319
Pocket greener and yellower, by often referring in conversation with me to my expectations; but here,

287
00:25:47,319 --> 00:25:53,639
again, he showed no consciousness, and even made it appear that he extorted—and even did extort,

288
00:25:53,641 --> 00:25:58,319
though I don’t know how—those references out of my innocent self.

289
00:25:58,319 --> 00:26:04,961
And when he and I were left alone together, he sat with an air upon him of general lying by in

290
00:26:04,961 --> 00:26:11,519
consequence of information he possessed, that really was too much for me. He cross-examined

291
00:26:11,519 --> 00:26:17,839
his very wine when he had nothing else in hand. He held it between himself and the candle, tasted the

292
00:26:17,839 --> 00:26:24,641
port, rolled it in his mouth, swallowed it, looked at his glass again, smelt the port, tried it,

293
00:26:24,641 --> 00:26:30,641
drank it, filled again, and cross-examined the glass again, until I was as nervous as if I had

294
00:26:30,641 --> 00:26:35,961
known the wine to be telling him something to my disadvantage. Three or four times I feebly thought

295
00:26:35,961 --> 00:26:41,398
I would start conversation; but whenever he saw me going to ask him anything, he looked at me with

296
00:26:41,398 --> 00:26:46,838
his glass in his hand, and rolling his wine about in his mouth, as if requesting me to take notice

297
00:26:46,839 --> 00:26:52,358
that it was of no use, for he couldn’t answer. I think Miss Pocket was conscious that the sight

298
00:26:52,358 --> 00:26:57,756
of me involved her in the danger of being goaded to madness, and perhaps tearing off her cap,—which

299
00:26:57,758 --> 00:27:03,839
was a very hideous one, in the nature of a muslin mop,—and strewing the ground with her hair,—which

300
00:27:03,839 --> 00:27:09,277
assuredly had never grown on her head. She did not appear when we afterwards went up to Miss

301
00:27:09,277 --> 00:27:17,277
Havisham’s room, and we four played at whist. In the interval, Miss Havisham, in a fantastic way,

302
00:27:17,277 --> 00:27:22,639
had put some of the most beautiful jewels from her dressing-table into Estella’s hair, and about her

303
00:27:22,641 --> 00:27:29,319
bosom and arms; and I saw even my guardian look at her from under his thick eyebrows, and raise them

304
00:27:29,319 --> 00:27:36,038
a little, when her loveliness was before him, with those rich flushes of glitter and colour in it.

305
00:27:36,038 --> 00:27:41,398
Of the manner and extent to which he took our trumps into custody, and came out with mean little

306
00:27:41,398 --> 00:27:48,199
cards at the ends of hands, before which the glory of our Kings and Queens was utterly abased,

307
00:27:48,199 --> 00:27:53,917
I say nothing; nor, of the feeling that I had, respecting his looking upon us personally in the

308
00:27:53,917 --> 00:28:01,277
light of three very obvious and poor riddles that he had found out long ago. What I suffered from,

309
00:28:01,277 --> 00:28:08,519
was the incompatibility between his cold presence and my feelings towards Estella. It was not that

310
00:28:08,519 --> 00:28:13,680
I knew I could never bear to speak to him about her, that I knew I could never bear to hear him

311
00:28:13,680 --> 00:28:20,038
creak his boots at her, that I knew I could never bear to see him wash his hands of her;

312
00:28:20,038 --> 00:28:24,038
it was, that my admiration should be within a foot or two of him,—it was,

313
00:28:24,038 --> 00:28:31,398
that my feelings should be in the same place with him,—that, was the agonizing circumstance.

314
00:28:31,398 --> 00:28:35,120
We played until nine o’clock, and then it was arranged that when Estella came

315
00:28:35,120 --> 00:28:40,199
to London I should be forewarned of her coming and should meet her at the coach;

316
00:28:40,199 --> 00:28:45,078
and then I took leave of her, and touched her and left her.

317
00:28:45,078 --> 00:28:51,038
My guardian lay at the Boar in the next room to mine. Far into the night, Miss Havisham’s words,

318
00:28:51,038 --> 00:28:57,038
“Love her, love her, love her!” sounded in my ears. I adapted them for my own repetition,

319
00:28:57,038 --> 00:29:04,000
and said to my pillow, “I love her, I love her, I love her!” hundreds of times. Then,

320
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:08,358
a burst of gratitude came upon me, that she should be destined for me,

321
00:29:08,358 --> 00:29:13,756
once the blacksmith’s boy. Then I thought if she were, as I feared,

322
00:29:13,758 --> 00:29:18,519
by no means rapturously grateful for that destiny yet, when would she begin

323
00:29:18,519 --> 00:29:25,718
to be interested in me? When should I awaken the heart within her that was mute and sleeping now?

324
00:29:25,718 --> 00:29:31,641
Ah me! I thought those were high and great emotions. But I never thought there was

325
00:29:31,641 --> 00:29:36,879
anything low and small in my keeping away from Joe, because I knew she would be contemptuous of

326
00:29:36,879 --> 00:29:43,559
him. It was but a day gone, and Joe had brought the tears into my eyes; they had soon dried,

327
00:29:43,559 --> 00:29:46,991
God forgive me! soon dried. Chapter 30.
